WEBVTT

NOTE
Generated by scripts/song-video/render.js from the song storyboard in _data/songs/; edit that file, not this one.

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:04.903
(narrator) Audio-described version: before each section, the picture pauses while I describe it.

2
00:00:04.903 --> 00:00:13.948
(narrator) The song is a comic opera on the stage of an opera house dressed as a software team's office: red velvet drapes, a gilded arch, footlights, and a conductor in the pit.

3
00:00:13.948 --> 00:00:21.855
(narrator) On the back wall are an arched window onto the city, a wall clock beside it, and a board headed Build status with six lamps, all green with ticks.

4
00:00:21.855 --> 00:00:35.557
(narrator) The curtain rises, and a follow spot picks up the Teammate, the tenor, gliding in from the left: a towering brown pompadour, a twirled moustache, a teal quarter-zip pullover over a white tee, studio headphones round his neck, jeans, and orange sneakers.

5
00:00:35.557 --> 00:00:39.619
(narrator) At a desk on the right, your hero, or the default Bruin, types at a laptop.

6
00:00:39.619 --> 00:00:42.435
(narrator) He raises a calming palm to them and pats his chest.

7
00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:45.793
(narrator) A sprint calendar sheds its fourteen days down to: ship today.

8
00:00:45.793 --> 00:00:52.238
(narrator) A toy ship flagged v1.0 by 3:00 is flown in from the rafters on two wires, the clock spins to three, and he salutes it.

9
00:00:52.238 --> 00:01:00.037
(narrator) Each teammate who answers him later is a woman with a face of her own, chosen at random and kept all song; at the very end your hero joins them.

10
00:01:00.037 --> 00:01:02.607
[music]

11
00:01:02.607 --> 00:01:08.237
♪ (Teammate) No need to worry! Just leave it to me! ♪

12
00:01:08.787 --> 00:01:15.497
♪ I'll save us a whole sprint— We'll ship it by three! ♪

13
00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:17.867
(narrator) Act one, Requirements.

14
00:01:17.867 --> 00:01:23.333
(narrator) A long scroll of requirements unrolls behind the Teammate, and he snaps it shut with one raised finger.

15
00:01:23.333 --> 00:01:30.763
(narrator) A giant phone running a ticket app, SeatSwap, shows a concert ticket, then one big Buy button, then the ticket locked to one name, gleaming.

16
00:01:30.763 --> 00:01:35.910
(narrator) Silhouetted hands in the audience go up with question marks; he shushes them and taps his temple.

17
00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:41.853
(narrator) A card for a meeting with users, What do they need?, appears, and he tears it in two and tosses the halves away.

18
00:01:41.853 --> 00:01:45.940
(narrator) He bows to a lit Applause sign, and the house stays silent but for a cricket.

19
00:01:46.553 --> 00:01:50.653
♪ Requirements? Requirements? Quite simple! Tickets: buy and go! ♪

20
00:01:50.653 --> 00:01:53.833
♪ No need to ask users— I'm certain I know! ♪

21
00:01:53.833 --> 00:01:56.613
♪ One ticket! One name! How sleek! How refined! ♪

22
00:01:56.613 --> 00:02:01.200
♪ I'll save them a meeting— They'll thank me in time! ♪

23
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:11.542
(narrator) The Product Manager glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a navy blazer over a coral tee, a lanyard and badge, grey trousers, white sneakers, and a clipboard.

24
00:02:11.542 --> 00:02:15.943
(narrator) The Teammate freezes with a sheepish grin and a bead of sweat while they sing.

25
00:02:15.943 --> 00:02:24.631
(narrator) A gold card headed User story writes itself out, part by part: As a fan, I want to pass my ticket on, so a friend can use my seat when I can't come along.

26
00:02:24.631 --> 00:02:26.211
(narrator) The phone answers: Transfer?

27
00:02:26.211 --> 00:02:26.888
(narrator) Not allowed.

28
00:02:26.888 --> 00:02:27.678
(narrator) One name only.

29
00:02:27.678 --> 00:02:31.740
(narrator) The Product Manager shows a card: Demo today, send a ticket to a friend.

30
00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:40.541
(narrator) Then the phone fans out into six small phones, red, orange, gold, green, blue, and violet, each with the same one button, and the Product Manager facepalms.

31
00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.520
♪ (Product Manager) As a fan, I want To pass my ticket on, ♪

32
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:48.180
♪ So a friend can use my seat When I can't come along! ♪

33
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.520
♪ I promised a demo Of what they could use. ♪

34
00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.833
♪ You've built the wrong product— In six lovely hues! ♪

35
00:02:56.133 --> 00:02:57.163
(narrator) Act two, Reuse.

36
00:02:57.163 --> 00:03:05.606
(narrator) A wall shelf of package boxes slides in: auth, charts, transfers, emoji, dates, and payments, each with its version number.

37
00:03:05.606 --> 00:03:10.342
(narrator) A sticker says Reuse!, and a red card says Build it anew: no, thanks.

38
00:03:10.342 --> 00:03:17.619
(narrator) The transfers package, version 1.4.2, flies off the shelf into the phone, which now sends a ticket to Sam.

39
00:03:17.619 --> 00:03:30.935
(narrator) He flicks away a padlocked document, package-lock.json, and new versions of the transfers package, 1.5.0, 2.0.0, and 2.1.0, march in from the right on little legs, each getting a sticker: fixes!

40
00:03:30.935 --> 00:03:33.475
(narrator) He holds a gold trophy high, beaming.

41
00:03:34.067 --> 00:03:38.687
♪ (Teammate) Transfers? Quite simple! I'll borrow the code! ♪

42
00:03:38.867 --> 00:03:42.197
♪ Design with reuse! Why build it anew? ♪

43
00:03:42.197 --> 00:03:45.127
♪ A package for transfers Will know what to do! ♪

44
00:03:45.127 --> 00:03:48.367
♪ No lockfile to fuss with! Let new versions in! ♪

45
00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:51.433
♪ New versions mean fixes— A sensible win! ♪

46
00:03:51.733 --> 00:04:01.060
(narrator) The Build Engineer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a hi-vis safety vest over a navy tee, olive cargo trousers, work boots, and a wrench.

47
00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.191
(narrator) The Teammate freezes again, sweating.

48
00:04:03.191 --> 00:04:10.099
(narrator) His laptop keeps the old transfers package, 1.4.2, while a blue build machine marked Clean build takes in version 2.1.0.

49
00:04:10.099 --> 00:04:16.778
(narrator) A plug for the old call, version one, bounces off a socket for version two's new call, with a spark and a red cross.

50
00:04:16.778 --> 00:04:23.860
(narrator) A lockfile card pins the old version, every lamp on the Build status board turns red, and a sticker says All builds failed.

51
00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:31.632
(narrator) Last, his laptop drops into a cardboard box marked Overnight, a label slaps on, Fragile: works on my machine, and he clutches his head.

52
00:04:32.227 --> 00:04:35.727
♪ (Build Engineer) Your laptop kept old ones; My clean build got new. ♪

53
00:04:35.727 --> 00:04:39.427
♪ Version two changed the API— Your calls won't go through! ♪

54
00:04:39.427 --> 00:04:43.227
♪ Next time, pin your versions! Till then, the builds fail. ♪

55
00:04:43.227 --> 00:04:48.433
♪ Shall I ship them your laptop By overnight mail? ♪

56
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:49.185
(narrator) Refrain.

57
00:04:49.185 --> 00:04:53.196
(narrator) The Teammate stands by his desk, where his laptop shows All tests pass.

58
00:04:53.196 --> 00:04:58.675
(narrator) He lifts it high on one palm in a golden light while the Build status board behind him stays red.

59
00:04:58.675 --> 00:05:03.307
(narrator) Back on the desk, it shows his code in a gilded frame, and he blows a chef's kiss.

60
00:05:03.307 --> 00:05:06.979
(narrator) A flip counter on the desk, Tiny fixes, counts 1, then 5, then 6.

61
00:05:06.979 --> 00:05:14.378
(narrator) Last, six lamps on his laptop's screen turn green, the footlights turn green, and he throws his arms up, while the board stays red.

62
00:05:14.983 --> 00:05:16.793
♪ (Teammate) It works on my machine! ♪

63
00:05:16.793 --> 00:05:21.253
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

64
00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:24.583
♪ One tiny fix— Or five or six— ♪

65
00:05:24.583 --> 00:05:27.403
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

66
00:05:28.433 --> 00:05:29.562
(narrator) Act three, Testing.

67
00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:37.109
(narrator) A code card, transfer.py, holds a function that checks the owner, gives the seat to a friend, notifies the friend, and logs it.

68
00:05:37.109 --> 00:05:41.744
(narrator) Gold test tags pin onto every line, and green numbers 1 to 4 onto their order.

69
00:05:41.744 --> 00:05:47.152
(narrator) A second card, test_transfer.py, asserts that the helpers are called in exactly that order.

70
00:05:47.152 --> 00:05:50.182
(narrator) A plaster mould closes round the code, sealed Cast.

71
00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:53.570
(narrator) The notify line flashes red, and a big red stamp says NO!

72
00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:54.283
(narrator) on the test.

73
00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.600
♪ I'll make this more solid! I'll test every call! ♪

74
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:06.990
♪ The order of helpers— I'll check it all! ♪

75
00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:14.080
♪ My implementation Is captured just so! ♪

76
00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:20.860
♪ If one small step changes, The tests will say "NO!" ♪

77
00:06:20.860 --> 00:06:25.633
[music]

78
00:06:25.633 --> 00:06:34.918
(narrator) The Test Engineer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a white lab coat over a lilac shirt, pens in the pocket, goggles round the neck, and a magnifying glass.

79
00:06:34.918 --> 00:06:36.517
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

80
00:06:36.517 --> 00:06:41.160
(narrator) In the code, two helpers are merged into one, and a last line says Sam now holds the seat.

81
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:46.783
(narrator) But the test card cracks and turns red, stamped Failed; night falls in the window, and the clock runs to two.

82
00:06:46.783 --> 00:06:52.457
(narrator) A sticker says Test the behavior, and the test rewrites itself to check only that Sam has the seat; it passes.

83
00:06:52.457 --> 00:07:01.071
(narrator) Then four little old test cards with faces cling to the bottom of the code with hearts, and the Test Engineer tugs at the code while the tests haul it back and shriek.

84
00:07:01.687 --> 00:07:05.647
♪ (Test Engineer) I merged two small helpers; The transfer worked right. ♪

85
00:07:05.647 --> 00:07:09.627
♪ Your brittle tests failed— There goes my whole night! ♪

86
00:07:09.627 --> 00:07:14.427
♪ Test what it does, not how: Then my friend's ticket should show! ♪

87
00:07:14.427 --> 00:07:19.527
♪ They're in love with your old code And won't let it go! ♪

88
00:07:20.233 --> 00:07:21.847
(narrator) Act four, Information hiding.

89
00:07:21.847 --> 00:07:26.297
(narrator) The Shows module is a little building with two doors, which he shoves wide open.

90
00:07:26.297 --> 00:07:30.135
(narrator) Inside is its table of shows, each with a showtime, like Friday 8 PM.

91
00:07:30.135 --> 00:07:39.759
(narrator) Three pages stand at the right as little houses, Tickets, Reminders, and Check-in; each reaches a long arm straight into the times, and each gets its own time math on a tag.

92
00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:44.822
(narrator) Then he switches the stored times to UTC, and the times change to Saturday 3 AM, and so on.

93
00:07:45.407 --> 00:07:48.267
♪ (Teammate) Information hiding? What's there to conceal? ♪

94
00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:51.647
♪ Each page reads the showtime Its own way! Ideal! ♪

95
00:07:51.647 --> 00:07:54.987
♪ Let every page do the math! It's simple! Direct! ♪

96
00:07:54.987 --> 00:07:58.633
♪ I'll switch them to UTC— What could that affect? ♪

97
00:07:58.933 --> 00:08:10.254
(narrator) The UI Developer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a mustard turtleneck under a denim painter's apron with dabs of paint and a brush in its pocket, rust trousers, and a palette.

98
00:08:10.254 --> 00:08:12.000
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

99
00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:15.492
(narrator) Tickets, Reminders, and Check-in crack, each with a red cross.

100
00:08:15.492 --> 00:08:23.546
(narrator) The times glow red, and dashed red lines race out to eight chips: receipts, emails, exports, reports, calendar, search, countdown, and scanner.

101
00:08:23.546 --> 00:08:29.066
(narrator) A gold card shows the fix, a Showtime module that alone hides the time format, and bandages go on.

102
00:08:29.066 --> 00:08:34.417
(narrator) As day and night race past the window, the UI Developer's hair turns silver and a cane appears.

103
00:08:35.007 --> 00:08:38.527
♪ (UI Developer) Now tickets, reminders, And check-in need repair! ♪

104
00:08:38.527 --> 00:08:42.097
♪ One detail has changed— It's used EVERYWHERE! ♪

105
00:08:42.097 --> 00:08:45.507
♪ Hide choices that change In one module— right? ♪

106
00:08:45.507 --> 00:08:49.433
♪ You moved every showtime— I've aged overnight! ♪

107
00:08:49.733 --> 00:08:50.152
(narrator) Refrain.

108
00:08:50.152 --> 00:08:59.773
(narrator) Again the Teammate lifts his laptop high on one palm in a golden light while the Build status board stays red, sets it back to show his code in a gilded frame, and blows a chef's kiss.

109
00:08:59.773 --> 00:09:02.440
(narrator) This time the Tiny fixes counter goes from 1 to 26.

110
00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:05.212
(narrator) His laptop's six lamps and the footlights turn green.

111
00:09:05.807 --> 00:09:08.767
♪ (Teammate) It works on my machine! ♪

112
00:09:08.767 --> 00:09:13.047
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

113
00:09:13.047 --> 00:09:15.347
♪ One tiny fix— Or twenty-six— ♪

114
00:09:15.347 --> 00:09:18.967
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

115
00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:20.736
(narrator) Act five, Shared history.

116
00:09:20.736 --> 00:09:26.612
(narrator) A commit graph shows shared main: an old base commit and four teammates' commits, each labelled fix.

117
00:09:26.612 --> 00:09:31.842
(narrator) Below it runs his wiggly branch of six small commits: wip, oops, fix, typo, wip2, and eh.

118
00:09:31.842 --> 00:09:33.664
(narrator) They fold into one tidy commit.

119
00:09:33.664 --> 00:09:39.540
(narrator) He force-pushes it onto main, knocking the four fix commits off the line, under a stamp: Force push.

120
00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:46.356
(narrator) He sweeps the fallen commits away with a broom, and a card says Code review: 1 commit to read, with a sticker, Easy!

121
00:09:46.957 --> 00:09:50.267
♪ I'll squash all my changes To one tidy line! ♪

122
00:09:50.267 --> 00:09:53.307
♪ Then force-push to shared main— That history will shine! ♪

123
00:09:53.307 --> 00:09:56.827
♪ One clean line of progress! Less clutter to see! ♪

124
00:09:56.827 --> 00:10:02.167
♪ Review will be easy— That's teamwork from me! ♪

125
00:10:02.467 --> 00:10:12.141
(narrator) The Back-End Developer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a charcoal hoodie with a little database on the chest, black jeans, sneakers, and a coffee mug.

126
00:10:12.141 --> 00:10:13.845
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

127
00:10:13.845 --> 00:10:16.648
(narrator) Dashed outlines show where their four commits were.

128
00:10:16.648 --> 00:10:22.474
(narrator) The old base commit gets cobwebs and a tag, old; every lamp on the board turns red, under a stamp: Broken.

129
00:10:22.474 --> 00:10:25.277
(narrator) A red card says Never force-push, then Merge first.

130
00:10:25.277 --> 00:10:31.269
(narrator) The fix commits drop back onto main after his, dawn breaks, the clock turns to six, and the lamps turn green.

131
00:10:31.269 --> 00:10:39.239
(narrator) The Back-End Developer points at him; two calendar cards, Saturday and Sunday, both free, drift up and vanish, and the Back-End Developer slumps.

132
00:10:39.847 --> 00:10:43.167
♪ (Back-End Developer) I pushed all our fixes! They're gone from shared main! ♪

133
00:10:43.167 --> 00:10:46.227
♪ You forced your OLD history— We're broken again! ♪

134
00:10:46.227 --> 00:10:49.907
♪ Never force-push! Merge first! I'll restore them by dawn. ♪

135
00:10:49.907 --> 00:10:54.467
♪ Some sprint that you saved us! My weekend is gone! ♪

136
00:10:54.767 --> 00:10:55.689
(narrator) Act six, Secrets.

137
00:10:55.689 --> 00:10:58.834
(narrator) Night falls, and the clock spins to a quarter to midnight.

138
00:10:58.834 --> 00:11:13.099
(narrator) He pats his chest and sends the Back-End Developer to bed: a bed appears behind them, they yawn, lie down in it under a purple checked quilt, and pull a sleep mask over their eyes, with Z's drifting up; then the bed glides off to the right with them asleep in it.

139
00:11:13.099 --> 00:11:19.390
(narrator) A dark card shows the key in .env, and in app.py the hard-coded key is struck out for one read from the environment.

140
00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:21.288
(narrator) A sticker says Safer!, and he bows.

141
00:11:21.288 --> 00:11:32.407
(narrator) Then a folder, my-app, holds app.py, a readme, and .env with a key; with git add, all three fly into a commit box, and with git push the box flies up into a cloud marked Public repo, where three keys hang.

142
00:11:32.407 --> 00:11:36.963
(narrator) Gold keys rain down from the cloud into the audience, whose hands reach up for them.

143
00:11:37.547 --> 00:11:40.897
♪ (Teammate) I'll take the deployment! You get some sleep! ♪

144
00:11:40.897 --> 00:11:43.947
♪ The keys live in dot-env, Not hard-coded now! ♪

145
00:11:43.947 --> 00:11:47.227
♪ A safer deployment— Allow me to bow! ♪

146
00:11:47.227 --> 00:11:50.687
♪ I add the whole folder; One push, and we're done! ♪

147
00:11:50.687 --> 00:11:55.900
♪ At last, a smooth handoff For everyone! ♪

148
00:11:56.200 --> 00:12:08.401
(narrator) The Security Engineer glides in from the right, with a woman's face of her own, a black tactical jacket with chest pockets, epaulettes, a gold shield badge, and an earpiece wire, black cargo trousers, and boots.

149
00:12:08.401 --> 00:12:10.194
(narrator) The Teammate freezes, sweating.

150
00:12:10.194 --> 00:12:15.514
(narrator) A card shows .gitignore without .env, and a red card shows Commit 4f2a, with the keys in it.

151
00:12:15.514 --> 00:12:18.347
(narrator) Two masked burglars grab the keys from the cloud.

152
00:12:18.347 --> 00:12:24.650
(narrator) Three server racks spin up, the board turns red, and a cloud bill climbs to minus 48,200 dollars, in the red.

153
00:12:24.650 --> 00:12:27.599
(narrator) A rocket marked Theirs, on bags of money, launches.

154
00:12:27.599 --> 00:12:34.711
(narrator) The Teammate adds .env to .gitignore, but the commit is still there, and he groans as five copies, each with a key, pop up.

155
00:12:34.711 --> 00:12:40.320
(narrator) The Security Engineer stamps their keys Rotated, and he points, aghast, as the bill climbs again.

156
00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:44.180
♪ (Security Engineer) Dot-env's not in gitignore— You committed the keys! ♪

157
00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:47.300
♪ Our PUBLIC repo hands them To strangers with ease! ♪

158
00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:50.800
♪ Thieves are spinning up servers; We're deep in the red! ♪

159
00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:54.540
♪ You wanted a launch— We're funding THEIRS instead! ♪

160
00:12:54.540 --> 00:12:56.140
♪ (Teammate) I'll add it to gitignore! ♪

161
00:12:56.140 --> 00:12:57.420
♪ (Security Engineer) That won't uncommit it! ♪

162
00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:58.540
♪ (Teammate) Old copies remain! ♪

163
00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.040
♪ (Security Engineer) Rotate those credentials— ♪

164
00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:01.333
♪ (Teammate) They're spending again! ♪

165
00:13:01.633 --> 00:13:02.022
(narrator) Finale.

166
00:13:02.022 --> 00:13:08.247
(narrator) All six teammates come back as a chorus line behind him, hands on hips; the UI Developer is still silver-haired.

167
00:13:08.247 --> 00:13:12.582
(narrator) He lifts his laptop high on one palm once more, then sets it back on the desk.

168
00:13:12.582 --> 00:13:17.751
(narrator) The Tiny fixes counter jumps from 1 to 96, and the six teammates facepalm, one after another.

169
00:13:17.751 --> 00:13:26.532
(narrator) His laptop's lamps and the footlights turn green, confetti bursts, and he holds the last note, arms high, while the teammates despair and the board stays red.

170
00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.480
♪ It works on my machine! ♪

171
00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:34.560
♪ The finest code you've seen! ♪

172
00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:37.880
♪ One tiny fix— Or ninety-six— ♪

173
00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:46.667
♪ And all the lights turn green! ♪

174
00:13:46.967 --> 00:13:47.283
(narrator) Outro.

175
00:13:47.283 --> 00:13:48.128
(narrator) The music stops.

176
00:13:48.128 --> 00:13:52.613
(narrator) His laptop now shows a red cross and the words Fatal error; he frowns at it, puzzled.

177
00:13:52.613 --> 00:13:56.149
(narrator) He steps to the desk and reaches for the keyboard with a sly smile.

178
00:13:56.149 --> 00:14:10.398
(narrator) Then all six teammates sing together: your hero, the worried teammate from the overture, in a blue hoodie with a gold star, glides in from the left between him and the desk, palm out, the six in the line-up hold out their palms too, and he jumps back with both hands up.

179
00:14:10.398 --> 00:14:16.414
(narrator) On the closing chord the curtain falls, and the moral of the opera is posted on it: Ask your users what they need.

180
00:14:16.414 --> 00:14:17.944
(narrator) Pin your dependency versions.

181
00:14:17.944 --> 00:14:19.739
(narrator) Test behavior, not implementation.

182
00:14:19.739 --> 00:14:21.427
(narrator) Hide decisions likely to change.

183
00:14:21.427 --> 00:14:23.169
(narrator) Never force-push a shared branch.

184
00:14:23.169 --> 00:14:24.435
(narrator) Keep secrets out of Git.

185
00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:29.280
Strange. It worked on my machine.

186
00:14:29.280 --> 00:14:32.540
I'll just make one small change.

187
00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:38.870
(All six teammates) Step away from the keyboard! Step away from the keyboard!

188
00:14:38.870 --> 00:14:42.200
[music]
